const sections = [
  ["What the public claim actually says", "LoloBuy’s public-facing service copy makes two specific, useful claims. It says products receive a comprehensive quality inspection in the warehouse and that inspection feedback is sent to the customer. It also describes global shipping. Those statements are valuable because they point to a moment when an item can be reviewed after it reaches the warehouse. They are not a promise that every concern will be noticed, that every image answers every question, or that shipping follows one universal timetable. A careful buyer treats the published statement as a starting point for an evidence check, not as a substitute for one."],
  ["Why warehouse feedback changes the decision", "A product listing is usually an intention: a chosen colour, size, variation and seller description. Warehouse feedback is different. It concerns a physical item that has arrived somewhere and can help a buyer compare the selection with what is visible at that moment. This distinction is practical. A buyer who saves the listing variation, price, sizing information and seller notes before ordering has something concrete to compare against later. Without that record, it becomes easy to remember what was expected rather than check what was selected. The most useful review starts with the order record, not with a general impression of a photograph."],
  ["Start with a record, not a feeling", "Before an order moves on, retain the product name, selected variation, size, colour, quantity and any seller-provided measurements. A saved screenshot or a plain written record is enough. When feedback is available, compare only observable details first. Is the colour broadly consistent under the available lighting? Is the count right? Does the visible label, shape or component match the selected variation? Are there obvious marks, damage or mismatched pieces? Writing these down turns the review into a repeatable process. It also stops a buyer from assuming that a small visual difference automatically means an error, or that a familiar-looking image automatically means everything is correct."],
  ["Ask narrow questions that can be answered", "If something needs clarification, ask a short, evidence-based question. ‘Is there a mark on the left shoe?’, ‘Can the measurement label be read?’, and ‘Is this the selected colourway?’ are more useful than ‘Is it good?’ A narrow question identifies the detail, the comparison and the decision point. It is easier for another person to understand and less likely to create a vague answer. This is particularly important when a product has variants with similar names or colours. Keep the original selection beside the feedback and avoid relying on memory alone. Good questions do not guarantee a desired answer; they make the available evidence more usable."],
  ["What warehouse images can establish", "Images can be useful evidence of visible identity and apparent condition at the time photographed. They may help confirm a readable label, an obvious colour difference, the number of visible items, packaging condition or a noticeable mark. That is already meaningful. A buyer can identify a mismatch early rather than discover it only after shipping. Still, an image should be described accurately. ‘The visible item appears consistent with the selected variation’ is a supportable conclusion. ‘The product is perfect’ is not. A practical guide gains trust by matching the strength of its claim to the strength of the evidence on screen."],
  ["What images cannot establish", "Warehouse photographs cannot reliably prove fit, material composition, comfort, long-term durability, authenticity, performance or every hidden defect. Camera angle, compression, lighting and a folded garment can change what a viewer sees. This is not a weakness unique to one service; it is a basic limit of remote visual review. For clothing, compare measurements in centimetres when they are available and allow for ordinary tolerance. For electronics or complex goods, do not infer performance from appearance alone. If the property that matters to you cannot be observed, the honest conclusion is that it remains unconfirmed. That answer may feel less satisfying, but it prevents confident-sounding content from becoming misleading."],
  ["Ship only after the item decision", "The same public service page refers to global shipping, but a global option is not the same as a guaranteed route, cost, tax treatment or delivery date. Those details depend on destination, parcel characteristics, current carrier choices and rules applying at the time. The sensible sequence is item decision first and parcel planning second. Before submitting anything for shipping, re-check the current service terms, destination eligibility, parcel weight and size, and any customs or tax information that applies to you. If a number matters to the decision, use the live order or shipping screen rather than an old screenshot, a search result or another buyer’s estimate."],
  ["A transparent buyer checklist", "Use this compact checklist: retain the original listing and selected options; compare feedback against the visible order record; note only observable mismatches; ask precise questions before the parcel stage; confirm any current shipping choices in the live service interface; and keep a record of the final decision. This process is deliberately unglamorous. Its value is that it creates a clear trail from what you chose to what you saw and what you decided. It can reduce avoidable misunderstandings, but it cannot remove the ordinary uncertainty of remote shopping or international shipping. That is why thoughtful research should explain limits alongside potential benefits."],
  ["What we intentionally do not claim", "This guide does not claim that LoloBuy serves a particular number of countries, provides a fixed delivery speed, charges a standard price, accepts every product, or guarantees a particular seller or item outcome. We could not verify those claims from the public material reviewed. That restraint is part of useful research. Good buying guidance tells readers what a source says, what an image can show and where the evidence stops. If a material question remains unanswered, pause and check the current service information before payment or parcel submission. Clear limits are more helpful than invented certainty."],
  ["The practical takeaway", "The best use of warehouse feedback is not to turn shopping into a confidence game. It is to make the next decision more explicit. Save the order details, inspect what is visible, identify the single question that matters and check current shipping terms only when the item decision is settled. LoloBuy’s public description supports the existence of warehouse inspection feedback and global shipping; the rest of a careful purchase decision still belongs to the buyer. Evidence before enthusiasm is slower for a minute, but usually clearer for the whole order."]
];
